C = [
("h1", "5. Romans 15:1–7: Bear, Please and Receive"),
("p", "Claim: Romans 15:1–7 gives the positive side of the limit set in chapter 14. The strong are to bear the infirmities of the weak and to please their neighbour for his good, and the ground is Christ’s own example. The passage ends with the instruction that opened chapter 14, to receive one another, and gives a new reason for it."),
("sub", "5.1 The strong bear"),
("p", "“We then that are strong ought to bear the infirmities of the weak, and not to please ourselves” (Romans 15:1). The first word, “then,” joins the sentence to what precedes it. Chapter 14 has told the strong what not to do. Chapter 15 tells them what they ought to do. The word “ought” is a debt, and the debt is to bear. “Infirmities” are weaknesses, and the weak man’s scruple is one. Paul does not tell the strong to remove the scruple, to argue it down or to correct it first. He tells them to bear it. The correction of the weak is not the subject of the passage; the conduct of the strong is."),
("sub", "5.2 Please the neighbour"),
("p", "“Let every one of us please his neighbour for his good to edification” (Romans 15:2). The pleasing is not flattery. It has an object, the neighbour’s good, and an end, edification. It is the same end Paul gave in Romans 14:19. The believer who seeks his neighbour’s good is not seeking his own, and Paul sets that against the thing he forbade in verse 1: “and not to please ourselves.” The limit on liberty is not a loss in the abstract. It is the exchange of one aim for another, the neighbour’s edification for one’s own pleasure."),
("sub", "5.3 Christ’s example"),
("p", "“For even Christ pleased not himself; but, as it is written, The reproaches of them that reproached thee fell on me” (Romans 15:3). The ground is the example of Christ. Paul quotes Scripture for the example, and the volume reports that he does and says no more about the passage he quotes. The point for this chapter is that Paul reaches for Christ’s own conduct as the reason for the strong man’s. The reason is not a principle of liberty. It is a person who gave up what he might have kept."),
("p", "“For whatsoever things were written aforetime were written for our learning, that we through patience and comfort of the scriptures might have hope” (Romans 15:4). The verse is a comment on the Scripture just quoted. It says that what was written aforetime was written for the learning of Paul’s readers, and it names three things the Scripture supplies: patience, comfort and hope. The volume does not draw from the verse any rule about which Old Testament passages are written to the Body. It reports the verse as Paul’s statement about the quoted verse and the use he makes of it."),
("sub", "5.4 One mind and one mouth"),
("p", "“Now the God of patience and consolation grant you to be likeminded one toward another according to Christ Jesus: that ye may with one mind and one mouth glorify God, even the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ” (Romans 15:5, 6). The wish is that the strong and the weak, who differ over meats and days, may be likeminded “according to Christ Jesus.” Paul does not ask them to agree about herbs. He asks them to glorify God with one mind and one mouth. The unity he prays for is a unity in worship across a difference of practice that he has already said he will leave."),
("sub", "5.5 Receive, as Christ received"),
("p", "“Wherefore receive ye one another, as Christ also received us to the glory of God” (Romans 15:7). The verse repeats the instruction of Romans 14:1, “receive ye,” and now it is mutual (“one another”) and it has a pattern, “as Christ also received us.” The reason given in 14:3 was that God had received the brother. The reason in 15:7 is that Christ received “us.” The end is “the glory of God.” The volume reads the verse as the close of the whole instruction on meats and days: the believer receives the brother, and the brother he receives is one Christ has received."),
("p", "The passage that follows (Romans 15:8–12) is not read in this volume. It speaks of Jesus Christ as a minister of the circumcision and of the Gentiles’ rejoicing, and the volume needs nothing from it for the instruction on receiving. The volume stops at verse 7, where the instruction on receiving ends, and says no more about what comes after."),
("ctbl", "Romans 15:1–7 by the five questions.", [
    ("Question", "Answer from the passage", "Verse"),
    ("Does Paul command?", "Yes, as a duty: the strong ought to bear and to please their neighbour", "15:1, 2"),
    ("What limit?", "Not to please ourselves; to seek the neighbour’s good to edification", "15:1, 2"),
    ("To whom is it owed?", "To the weak, and to the neighbour", "15:1, 2"),
    ("What ground?", "Christ pleased not himself; Christ received us to the glory of God", "15:3, 7"),
    ("What is unsaid?", "How the weak are to grow; what is to be borne in each case", "Chapter 16"),
  ], [0.26, 0.52, 0.22]),
("sub", "5.6 What the volume takes from the passage"),
("bul", [
    "The strong ought to bear the infirmities of the weak and not to please themselves.",
    "The aim is the neighbour’s good to edification, and the ground is Christ’s own example.",
    "The instruction ends where it began, with “receive,” and now the pattern is how Christ received us.",
    "The volume reads Romans 15:1–7 and stops, leaving verses 8 to 12 to the places where they are read.",
  ]),
]
